    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t02_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Mohr's salt is FeSO4·(NH4)2SO4·6H2O, so it has 6 water molecules of crystallisation."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t02_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Dilute H2SO4 suppresses the hydrolysis of Fe2+ ions, which would otherwise make the solution turbid."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t02_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "94.8/948 = 0.1 mol alum. Each formula unit has 4 SO4^2-, so 0.1 × 4 = 0.4 mol."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t02_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Prolonged heating promotes air oxidation of Fe2+ to Fe3+, which gives a yellowish product. Both statements are true and R explains A."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t02_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "blind_working": "Alum gives 2K+ : 2Al3+ : 4SO4^2-, which is 1:1:2, so I is correct. Mohr's salt resists air oxidation much better than FeSO4 and is used as a primary standard, so II is correct as intended, though 'stable' slightly overstates it."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t03_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Benzenediazonium chloride couples with aniline at the para position. This gives p-aminoazobenzene, which is aniline yellow."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t03_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Free -NH2 activates the ring so strongly that it gives polysubstitution, and it is easily oxidised by HNO3. Acetylation moderates this activation, protects the -NH2, and favours para substitution."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t03_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Propan-2-ol has a CH3CH(OH)- group. It is oxidised to acetone, which gives the iodoform test. Methanol, propan-1-ol and pentan-3-one do not."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t03_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Acetanilide is white, m.p. about 114 °C (II). p-Nitroacetanilide is pale yellow, m.p. about 214 °C (IV). Aniline yellow comes from diazo coupling (III). Iodoform is yellow with an antiseptic smell, m.p. about 119 °C (I)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t03_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true. B is false: the p-isomer crystallises from ethanol and the o-isomer stays in the filtrate. C is true. D is true: KI with NaOCl generates the hypoiodite needed. E is false: 4 mol I2 are used per mole of ethanol (1 for oxidation to acetaldehyde, 3 for iodination). So A, C and D are correct."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t04_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Weak acid vs strong base gives an equivalence point near pH 8-9. The pH jump lies in the basic range, so phenolphthalein (pH 8.2-10) is suitable."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t04_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "1.26/126 = 0.01 mol in 250 mL, so 0.04 M. 25 mL holds 0.001 mol of the diprotic acid, needing 0.002 mol NaOH. 0.002/0.020 L = 0.10 M."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t04_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Weak base vs strong acid has its pH jump in the acidic range, so methyl orange is used. It is yellow in the basic flask and turns red at the end point."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t04_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true: only a few drops are added so the indicator does not noticeably affect the titration. R is false: indicators are weak organic acids or bases and do react (they are themselves titrated)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t04_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "I is correct: the strong acid-strong base jump is about pH 3-11, so both indicators work. II is correct: the strong acid-weak base jump is acidic (about pH 3-7), so methyl orange works."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t05_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Dilute H2SO4 is used because it does not react with KMnO4. HCl is oxidised to Cl2, and HNO3 is itself an oxidising agent."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t05_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Equivalents of Fe2+ = 20 × 0.1 × 1 = 2 meq. KMnO4 normality = 0.02 × 5 = 0.1 N, so V = 2/0.1 = 20 mL."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t05_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Oxalic acid gives 20 × 0.05 × 2 = 2 meq. Setting 25 × M × 5 = 2 gives M = 0.016 M."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t05_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Equivalent weight of KMnO4 in acid = 158/5 = 31.6 (I). n-factor of oxalic acid = 2 (II). n-factor of Fe2+ = 1 (III). n-factor of KMnO4 in acid = 5 (IV)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u20_t05_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true and B is true. C is false: oxalic acid is warmed, not Mohr's salt, since Fe2+ would be air-oxidised. D is false: KMnO4 is not a primary standard. E is true: Mn2+ autocatalyses the reaction."
    },
    {
